The Security Council,
Recalling all its relevant resolutions concerning Yemen and the Red Sea, including resolutions 2216 (2015), 2722 (2024) and 2739 (2024),
Reaffirming its respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence and unity of Yemen and of the coastal States of the Red Sea, and its commitment to international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea,
Condemning in the strongest terms the continued Houthi attacks from Yemen against merchant and commercial vessels, including missile, drone and unmanned surface vessel attacks, and attacks against civilian infrastructure and the territory of States in the region,
Expressing grave concern at the threat these attacks pose to maritime security, freedom of navigation, the safety of seafarers, and regional and global supply chains, food and energy security,
Recalling the right of Member States to defend their vessels from attacks in accordance with international law, including the Charter of the United Nations and the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea,
Reaffirming the targeted arms embargo imposed by resolution 2216 (2015) and emphasizing the obligation of all Member States to comply fully with it,
Expressing concern at the humanitarian situation in Yemen, including the need for rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access, and at the detention of United Nations personnel and humanitarian workers by the Houthis,
Reaffirming the need for a comprehensive political solution to the conflict in Yemen and expressing support for the United Nations-led peace process under the auspices of the Secretary-General and his Special Envoy,
Stressing the need to avoid further escalation in the Red Sea and the broader region, and encouraging enhanced diplomatic efforts to address root causes, including conflicts contributing to regional tensions,
1. Condemns in the strongest terms the continued Houthi attacks from Yemen against merchant and commercial vessels, including the attack on the M/V Tutor, and against civilian infrastructure and the territory of States in the region;
2. Demands that the Houthis immediately cease all attacks against merchant and commercial vessels and all attacks against civilian infrastructure, and refrain from further destabilizing acts in the Red Sea and the wider region;
3. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of the M/V Galaxy Leader and its crew, as well as all United Nations personnel and humanitarian workers detained by the Houthis;
4. Reiterates that all Member States must fully implement the targeted arms embargo contained in resolution 2216 (2015), and calls upon all Member States to strengthen efforts, in accordance with their obligations under international law, to prevent the transfer of arms and related materiel to the Houthis in violation of that embargo;
5. Reaffirms the right of Member States to defend their vessels from attacks in accordance with international law, and calls for continued cooperation and coordination among Member States and regional organizations to protect freedom of navigation and maritime security;
6. Urges all parties to exercise maximum restraint, avoid further escalation, respect international humanitarian law, protect civilians, and ensure safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access throughout Yemen;
7. Reiterates the need to address the root causes of the conflict and instability, including the conflict in Yemen and broader regional tensions, and calls upon all Yemeni parties and relevant regional and international actors to engage constructively with the United Nations-led peace process, including the Special Envoy, and to support a comprehensive political settlement;
8. Requests the Secretary-General to continue reporting to the Council on a monthly basis, and to include in those reports information on the implementation of this resolution, attacks on vessels, the humanitarian situation, arms embargo implementation, and detentions, until 15 July 2025;
9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling all its relevant resolutions concerning Yemen and the Red Sea, including resolutions 2216 (2015), 2722 (2024) and 2739 (2024),

Reaffirming its respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence and unity of Yemen and of the coastal States of the Red Sea, the principle of non-intervention in the internal affairs of States, and its commitment to international law, including the Charter of the United Nations,

Condemning in the strongest terms the continued Houthi attacks from Yemen against merchant and commercial vessels, including missile, drone and unmanned surface vessel attacks, and attacks against civilian infrastructure and the territory of States in the region,

Expressing grave concern at the threat these attacks pose to maritime security, freedom of navigation, the safety of seafarers, and regional and global supply chains, food and energy security,

Reaffirming the need for an immediate and durable ceasefire in Gaza and for all parties to comply with their obligations under international law and relevant Security Council resolutions, and recognizing the clear link between the situation in Gaza and the escalation of hostilities in the Red Sea and the broader region,

Recalling that any measures taken by Member States to protect their vessels from attacks must be in accordance with international law, including the Charter of the United Nations and the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, and must fully respect the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Yemen and other coastal States and avoid further escalation,

Reaffirming the targeted arms embargo imposed by resolution 2216 (2015) and emphasizing the obligation of all Member States to comply fully with it,

Expressing concern at the humanitarian situation in Yemen, including the need for rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access, and at the detention of United Nations personnel and humanitarian workers by the Houthis,

Reaffirming the need for a comprehensive political solution to the conflict in Yemen and expressing support for the United Nations-led peace process under the auspices of the Secretary-General and his Special Envoy,

Stressing the need to avoid further escalation in the Red Sea and the broader region, and encouraging enhanced diplomatic efforts to address root causes, including conflicts contributing to regional tensions,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the continued Houthi attacks from Yemen against merchant and commercial vessels, including the attack on the M/V Tutor, and against civilian infrastructure and the territory of States in the region;

2. Demands that the Houthis immediately cease all attacks against merchant and commercial vessels and all attacks against civilian infrastructure, and refrain from further destabilizing acts in the Red Sea and the wider region;

3. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of the M/V Galaxy Leader and its crew, as well as all United Nations personnel and humanitarian workers arbitrarily detained by the Houthis, including the staff of the United Nations Human Rights Office in Sanaa, and to respect the inviolability of United Nations premises;

4. Reiterates that all Member States must fully implement the targeted arms embargo contained in resolution 2216 (2015), and calls upon all Member States to strengthen efforts, in accordance with their obligations under international law, including the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea and respect for flag State jurisdiction, to prevent the transfer of arms and related materiel to the Houthis in violation of that embargo; such efforts should be carried out through dialogue and cooperation, in full respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Yemen and other States in the region, and in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations;

5. Calls upon all Member States to ensure that any measures taken to protect their vessels from attacks are in full conformity with international law, including the Charter of the United Nations and the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, and with full respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Yemen and the coastal States of the Red Sea; stresses that nothing in this resolution authorizes the use of force against the territory of any State; further stresses that such measures must be proportionate, avoid further escalation, and be consistent with the strict conditions of self-defence under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law; and calls for continued cooperation and coordination among Member States, including through the International Maritime Organization, and regional organizations to protect freedom of navigation and maritime security;

6. Calls upon the international community to support the coastal States of the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden, including through capacity-building, to enhance maritime domain awareness, combat piracy and armed robbery at sea, and protect critical maritime infrastructure;

7. Urges all parties to exercise maximum restraint, avoid further escalation, respect international humanitarian law and international human rights law, protect civilians, including humanitarian and medical personnel and civilian infrastructure, ensure full, safe, rapid and unhindered humanitarian access throughout Yemen, including for humanitarian personnel, and ensure accountability for violations; and calls upon all Member States to refrain from military actions that exacerbate tensions and the humanitarian situation in Yemen and the region;

8. Reiterates the need to address the root causes of the conflict and instability, including the conflict in Yemen, the situation in the Gaza Strip and broader regional tensions, and calls upon all Yemeni parties and relevant regional and international actors to engage constructively with the United Nations-led peace process, including the Special Envoy, and to support a comprehensive, inclusive, Yemeni-led and Yemeni-owned political settlement based on agreed references, with the full, equal and meaningful participation of women;

9. Requests the Secretary-General to continue reporting to the Council on a monthly basis, and to include in those reports information on the implementation of this resolution, attacks on vessels, the safety and welfare of seafarers and the impact on freedom of navigation, the humanitarian and human rights situation, the protection of civilians, civilian casualties and damage to civilian infrastructure caused by military operations, arms embargo implementation, detentions, and regional developments, including the situation in Gaza, until 15 July 2025;

10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

